Description
===========

This is the P600FW project, a CPU/firmware remake for the SCI Prophet 600 (http://www.vintagesynth.com/sci/p600.php).
It uses a slightly modified Teensy++ board (http://www.pjrc.com/store/teensypp_pins.html) that plugs into the Z80 socket.

Installation and usage
======================

See documentation...

History
=======
- all revisions based on Gligli V2.1 RC3
- V2.20 JS - added noise mod and new LFO waveforms step8, seq, RevSaw
- V2.21 JS - added new LFO waveforms step4
- V2.22 JS - added 4bit Triangle and 6bit Sine
- V2.23 JS - fixed patch compatibility bug introduced in V 2.22
- V2.24 JS - reverted polymod bug to RC1 behavior 
- V2.25 JS - added new vibrato waveforms and Vibrato targets
- V2.26B JS - fixed post “Tune” volume bug introduced in V2.25
- V2.28 JS - added polymod VCO A, VCO B, VCO A-B targets
- V2.29 JS - fixed error with CC 57 and 74 (LFO Shape and Vibe Shape) where not all shapes were selectable, sped up UI to V2.26B speed, Added CC 77 (Poly Mod Filter Env to modulate VCO A, VCO B, or VCO AB)
- V2.31 JS - added filter resonance as a vibrato target and increased range on the noise (or CV in for an unmodified P600) target for the vibrato
- V2.32 JS - tried to speed up volume knob
- V2.33 JS - Polymod env target PWM VCOA and VCOB, fixed bug introduced in V2.32 causing volume to drop over time
- V2.34 JS - JAM revised Arp to remove double notes and added new random mode (Thanks!!) The new random mode prevent notes to play several times in a row; each note is always different than the previous one (except if only one note is played, obviously).
- V2.5 - the Imogen/Flat-Labs firmware with all the fixes and features of the Flat-Labs fork (arp Euclid/probability/swing/resolution/ratchets, ensemble vibrato, 12 LFO shapes, vibrato shape/range/8 targets, poly-mod envelope targets, aftertouch, speed work, preset validation) on the Teensy++ 2.0. Loads V2.34 patches (see "V2.5" below)


V2.5
====

V2.5 is the firmware of the Flat-Labs Z80P600 fork (Imogen's 2022 firmware plus the fork's fixes and features) running on the Teensy++ 2.0,
with V2.34's Teensy hardware layer (pins, USB, flash storage, sysex updater). Everything V2.34 added is in it as well (12 LFO shapes, vibrato
shape/range/targets, noise level, bender PW and VCO B, poly-mod envelope to VCO A/B/both/PW, aftertouch, CC7/120/123, JAM's random arp).
NOTHING OF THIS WAS TESTED ON A REAL INSTRUMENT. It was tested in a simulator that runs the real firmware on an emulated AT90USB1286 (see the end).

What is different from V2.34
----------------------------
- The menus are Imogen's. The parameter of a keypad changes with every press of that keypad (To Tape + keypad in preset mode):

  | Key | 1st press | 2nd press | 3rd press | more presses |
  |-----|-----------|-----------|-----------|--------------|
  | 0 | arp/seq speed | arp Euclid rhythm | arp probability | 4 swing, 5 resolution, 6 ratchet, 7 ratchet probability |
  | 1 | LFO shape (pairs) | LFO target | LFO trigger/sync | |
  | 2 | vibrato speed | vibrato amount | vibrato target (8) | 4 ensemble, 5 vibrato shape, 6 vibrato range |
  | 3 | mod wheel target | mod delay | mod wheel range | |
  | 4 | pitch (free/semi/oct...) | ext volt (= V2.34 "noise") | pulse bug | |
  | 5 | filter env shape | 2nd env shape | env routing | 4 poly-mod env target |
  | 6 | bender target | bender range | | |
  | 7 | assigner priority | assign mode | glide (GliGli panel layout) | |
  | 8 | unison detune | vintage (spread) | drive (SCI panel layout) | |
  | 9 | amp velocity | filter velocity | | |

  V2.34's "To Tape + 1/2/3 three times" (vibrato shape, range, targets) are now To Tape + 2 (press 5, 6 and 3).
- From Tape functions: 0 local on/off, 1 MIDI receive channel, 2 MIDI send channel, 3 bender calibration, 4/5 voice select/defeat,
  6 panel layout (GliGli or SCI), 7 boot light show on/off, 8 sync mode, 9 VCF limit, Preset = basic patch, Seq1 = reset the settings (retunes).
  **From Tape + 6 no longer dumps the presets.** Patch management is From Tape + Record: then Preset dumps all presets, Record leaves.
- MIDI CC numbers are the ones of the Flat-Labs fork (cc_list_v34_vs_now.md has the full list). Different from V2.34: CC 48-50 are mix vol A /
  glide-vol B / drive, CC 57 LFO shape (12 values), CC 58 unused, CC 66-79 follow the new parameter order, vibrato shape is CC 115 and vibrato
  range CC 116 (V2.34: CC 74/75). The Ctrlr panel for V2.34 does not match V2.5.
- LFO shapes: step4 and step8 run at the speed of saw (V2.34: 4x and 8x slower), step4 has 4 levels (V2.34: 2), bit sine 16 levels (V2.34: 64).

Storage on the Teensy
---------------------
V2.5 is about 77 KB of program. V2.34 kept presets in flash from 0x10000 (64 KB) up, which V2.5 needs for its code, so the storage area is now
0x16000-0x1dfff (128 pages): presets 0-99, sequencer 104-105, manual preset 123, settings 124-125. The sequencer pages, the manual preset and the
settings (with the tuning) are at the SAME flash addresses as in V2.34. The Makefile stops the build if the program would reach 0x16000
(about 12.6 KB are free now).

Getting your V2.34 patches into V2.5
------------------------------------
V2.5 recognises V2.34 patches (also V2.20-V2.33) and converts them when they are loaded or received by sysex:
1. On V2.34, dump all presets: From Tape + 6, record the sysex on the computer.
2. Install V2.5 (below).
3. On V2.5: From Tape + Record (patch management), send the recorded sysex, press Record to leave.
   Each patch is converted and stored in V2.5's format.
The conversion: the shared part (oscillators, filter, envelopes, LFO, voice pattern, per-note tuning) is read like a GliGli 2.1 patch, which also
converts the LFO speed/amount, vibrato speed/amount and envelope times to Imogen's curves. Then: noise level -> ext volt (same voltage), vibrato
shape, range and target, poly-mod envelope target, bender target (V2.34 PW and VCO B have other numbers here), mod wheel range (V2.34 stored a shift),
pitch mode 3 (CC only) -> oct, pulse bug off (V2.34 behaviour). Patches made before V2.25 get a triangle vibrato at normal range (GliGli's vibrato),
not pulse/low as V2.34 played them. A V2.34 patch is recognised by data V2.34 writes twice; a V2.5 patch with mod delay, vibrato speed/amount,
detune, external level, spread, routing, assign, LFO trigger and name all zero could in theory be taken for one, nothing else.

Installing
----------
- **Teensy Loader (USB), recommended**: flash "p600firmware V2.5.hex". Count on the presets, settings and sequences being erased (dump the presets first). Tune after.
  It also installs the new updater (see below).
- **Sysex update from V2.34**: power on with From Tape + To Tape held, send "p600firmware V2.5.syx". The display stops spinning when the
  transfer is done, it does NOT show 'S' (see below): wait until your MIDI program has sent everything, then switch off and on.
  Kept: settings and tuning, sequences, manual preset (converted). Lost: presets 0-95 (V2.5's code is where they were). V2.34's presets 96-99
  appear as V2.5 presets 0-3, the rest are empty until you send your dump (above).
- Why no 'S': the updater in the Teensy is never replaced by a sysex update, and V2.34's updater computes its checksum with a function
  inside the program it is replacing (at 0x61a). The .syx sends that page last and leaves out the end message, so the old function is used
  until the end. A .syx in the usual order makes V2.34's updater crash in the middle and write garbage (simulator), and the Teensy then
  needs the Teensy Loader. V2.5's own updater (installed with the Teensy Loader) has its own checksum function and works with both orders.

Fixes in the Teensy part
------------------------
- Startup: the code that runs before main() jumped over the copy of the initial values of variables (menu texts, tables) when the compiler
  puts that copy first (avr-gcc 7.3 does; the V2.34 hex was built with a compiler that does not). It now runs the normal startup.
- Updater: own CRC function (above).
- A sysex patch dump for a number above 99 is ignored (on the Teensy those pages are the sequencer, manual preset and settings).

Tested (simulator only)
-----------------------
simavr with an AT90USB1286 core and the P600 bus decoded from the Teensy pins, flash writes through the Teensy bootloader SPM call, a 6850 UART at
MIDI speed. The V2.34 data was made by the real V2.34 firmware in the same simulator.
- Boots, main loop and 2 kHz interrupt run; interrupt load idle 5296 of 8000 cycles (Flat-Labs board 5295), 6 voices + LFO + vibrato 6840 (7010),
  unison + wide ensemble + LFO to VCO/VCF/PW worst tick 8581 (8771): this case still overruns the tick slot, as on the Flat-Labs board.
- 12 scenarios compared with the Flat-Labs build (288000 S&H values): equal except start-up transients and the bender-to-VCA volume, which the
  main loop writes and whose timing differs between the boards.
- V2.34 dump of 24 patches sent over MIDI: all parameters as expected by an independent model. Sysex update from V2.34 image: settings/tuning,
  manual preset, presets 96-99 and both sequencer tracks as expected. 300 random V2.5 patches (and as versions 8-12): none taken for V2.34.
- Sysex update with V2.34's updater: program identical to V2.5, storage unchanged; with the usual order it fails (as explained above).
  V2.5's updater: both orders work, shows 'S'. The V2.5 program after the update boots and runs.
- Menu texts/cycles (65 checks), all stepped CCs, save/load roundtrip, vibrato/LFO targets and ranges (141 checks).
- 13 deliberate faults in the new code (converter mappings, recognition, page guard, startup jump): every one makes a test fail.
Not tested: anything on hardware (sound, timing of the real bus, the display, the Teensy bootloader call, MIDI hardware).


V2.25 includes -
LFO has 6 new waveforms:
Step8, RevSaw, Seq, Step4, Bit_Tri, Bit_Sine
New Pitch Bender targets:PW(VCO A), and VCO B only 

New noise source setting control. Pressing "to tape" 0 a second time lets you adjust the VCF CV input level and store it with a patch. I modified my P600 to add a noise source and use this parameter to adjust the noise source per patch, hence in the UI this displays as "noise". However, in an unmodified Prophet 600, this parameter controls the VCF CV input level.

Vibrato now has: 1) all LFO waveforms (CC74), 2) high and low range (CC75), and 3) target AB, A, B, VCA, Noise level, and Resonance (CC76)

Added channel aftertouch (e.g., channel pressure). If you use an external controller that has aftertouch controlling the Prophet 600, the Prophet 600 now uses this MIDI channel aftertouch data to modulate whatever the Mod-wheel is modulating. For example, I used it to apply vibrato to notes.

Added Poly-Aftertouch (e.g., per note)

Also added are:
CC7 Volume 
CC120 (All sound off)
CC123 (All notes off)

To use vibrato waveforms "to tape" + 1 three times

To use vibrato range "to tape" + 2 three times

To use vibrato targets "to tape" + 3 three times

To use the New Random Mode Arp: press assign button twice


